# nr-tui-md-format: what the wiring applies (crate crates/mixdog-markdown-ansi gains the formatter, the segment renderer and three dependencies)

The crate is already a workspace member and a `[workspace.dependencies]` entry (nr-tui-md-lexer); nothing is registered and no
protocol method is added.

## native/core/Cargo.toml

Nothing: `mixdog-highlight`, `mixdog-termtext` and `mixdog-tui-style` are already workspace members and `[workspace.dependencies]`
entries; the crate's own manifest now names them as `{ workspace = true }`.

## native/core/Cargo.lock

Package `mixdog-markdown-ansi`: its `dependencies` list becomes (alphabetical, three entries added)

    "mixdog-highlight",
    "mixdog-js",
    "mixdog-termtext",
    "mixdog-tui-style",
    "mixdog-workspace-hack",
    "regress",
    "serde",
    "serde_json",
    "sha2 0.10.9",

No cycle: none of the three crates depends on mixdog-markdown-ansi. Every crate is already in the lock.

## native/core/crates/mixdog-workspace-hack/Cargo.toml

Nothing.

## compose_flip.rs / protocol tables

Nothing (library code of the terminal UI; no service).

## The golden the twin test reads

`tests/goldens/format.json` is recorded (node tests/recorders/record-format.mjs --tree C:/Project/mixdog --write ...) and was
checked again with `--check` ("format golden matches"). The Rust side could not be compiled or run in the workspace (no cargo
in this task): the twin test `tests/format_twin.rs` is what compares this crate's bytes with the recording.

## What the next tasks build on

- `mixdog_markdown_ansi::render::{render_token_ansi_segments, RenderOptions, Segment}`: the blocks of a text, a table block
  carrying its token for `table::build_table_render` (nr-tui-md-table) and the others their ANSI string.
- `mixdog_markdown_ansi::render::render_markdown_ansi(text, columns)` calls `table::build_table_render` for a table block; until
  that function is filled in (it is still the `unimplemented!` stub) a text with a table panics there.
- `mixdog_markdown_ansi::format::{format_token, format_block, Context, styles, ExtraColorizers, highlight_code_line,
  highlight_code_block_to_lines, colorize_diff_line, looks_like_unified_diff, lang_family, pad_aligned}`: what `tool-output-format.mjs`
  and the table's `padAligned` consumers read from `format-token.mjs`. `pad_aligned` (`padAligned`) is in the same module.

## Left for nr-tui-md-stream (render.rs is this task's file; the functions it needs are that task's)

`lexMarkdown(content, { trimPartialFences: true })` of `render-ansi.mjs` is not complete in `render::lex_markdown`: its
`trim_partial_fences` branch lexes the text fresh (not cached) but does not yet (1) take the open-fence fast path
(`findOpenFenceStart(text)`: lex only the text before the open fence and push a `TokenKind::Code` token built from the raw
fence text with `lang` = the fence's language, `text` = everything after the fence line's newline (empty without one),
`raw` = the fence onward, `plain = Some(true)`) or (2) call `trimPartialClosingFences(tokens)` on the result. Both functions
are `stream-fence.mjs`, which nr-tui-md-stream ports into `stream.rs`; the formatter already renders a `plain` code token
through the flat streaming body path. The lead applies this two-call change to `lex_markdown` with that task's functions.